course: myths
lesson: 28
title: Where did you read that? Checking sources
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Finn holds up his tablet: "It says on a website, so it MUST be true!" Isla waves Grandad's old encyclopaedia: "Nine planets, it's printed right here!" Both are trusting the container, not checking the claim.
explain: Every fact comes from somewhere, and a good checker asks four questions: Who says it? How do they know? When was it written? What do other good sources say?
explain: A printed book can be out of date, a headline can squash a small study into a big claim, an advert wants to sell you something, and "my brother's mate's dad" is a chain nobody can check.
explain: Wikipedia is a good place to start, because it lists its sources at the bottom, but anyone can edit it, so follow the links; the same goes for doctors on YouTube.
explain: Checking isn't sneering: Rhys's zoo sign about polar bears is roughly right, and finding that out is part of the game too.
fact: Searing meat (cooking its outside fast on a very hot pan until brown) does not "seal in the juices"; seared meat loses as much moisture as unseared meat, but browning adds flavour.
fact: Pluto was called a planet until 2006, when astronomers made it a dwarf planet, so books printed before then list nine planets.
fact: "Clinically proven" (tested on people) in an advert can mean a tiny test without fair comparison; ask who tested it, on how many people, and compared with what.
fact: Poinsettias are not deadly; eating them may cause a mild tummy upset, and very large studies of poisoning calls found no deaths from them.
fact: Wikipedia is a useful starting point that lists its sources, but anyone can edit it, so check the sources it links to.
fact: The idea that "Eskimos have 100 words for snow" grew as it was retold; Inuit and Yupik languages build long words from parts, so counting their "words" for snow is not simple.
fact: One person with a medical title saying something odd online is not the same as what most doctors agree on; vegetables are healthy food.
fact: Headlines like "Chocolate makes you clever!" often come from a small study, sometimes on animals, and the article usually says much less than the headline.
fact: A polar bear's fur looks white, but each hair is actually colourless and the skin underneath is black.
fact: The Apollo Moon landings really happened between 1969 and 1972; evidence includes Moon rocks, mirrors left on the Moon that scientists still bounce lasers off, and tracking by other countries.
fact: A story passed through "my brother's mate's dad" cannot be checked and is weak evidence.
fact: Books and encyclopaedias can be wrong or out of date, so check when they were written.
exercise: Does searing meat seal in the juices? | No, but browning adds flavour | Yes, it locks in every drop | Yes, but only for red meat | No, it makes the meat raw inside
exercise: Why does Grandad's old encyclopaedia list nine planets? | It was printed before Pluto became a dwarf planet in 2006 | A tenth planet was destroyed after it was printed | Encyclopaedias always add one extra planet | The ninth planet was hidden from new books
exercise: What should you ask about a "clinically proven" advert? | Who tested it, on how many people, compared with what | What colour the bottle is | How famous the person in the advert is, and how much they smile | How much the advert cost to make
exercise: Are poisonous poinsettias deadly? | No, at worst a mild tummy upset | Yes, one leaf can kill a child | Yes, but only at Christmas time | Only the red ones, not the white ones
exercise: How should you use Wikipedia? | As a starting point, then check its sources | As the final word on everything, never wrong | Never, because everything on it is always false | Only for its pictures, never for any of its facts
exercise: Why is "Eskimos have 100 words for snow" misleading? | Their languages build long words from parts, so counting is not simple | They have no words for snow at all | They have exactly one word for snow | Nobody has ever studied their languages
exercise: A doctor on YouTube says vegetables are poison. What should you do? | Check what most doctors agree on | Believe it, because he is a doctor | Stop eating vegetables at once | Share it with all your friends
exercise: A headline says "Chocolate makes you clever!" What should you do? | Read the article and find out how small the study was | Eat as much chocolate as you possibly can before every test | Believe it because it's in a newspaper | Ignore all newspapers forever
exercise: What colour are a polar bear's hairs really? | Colourless, though the fur looks white | Bright white, painted that colour by the snow | Black all over, the same colour as its skin | Yellow, but bleached white by the Arctic sun
exercise: What colour is a polar bear's skin? | Black | White | Pink | Blue
exercise: What evidence shows the Moon landings happened? | Moon rocks, mirrors on the Moon and tracking by other countries | A film made in a studio | A poster in the NASA shop | A friend of a friend who works there
exercise: How strong is a claim from "my brother's mate's dad"? | Weak, because nobody can check it | Strong, because he works somewhere important | Perfect, because it came through three people | Strong, if your brother believes it
exercise: Can a printed book be wrong? | Yes, it can be wrong or out of date | No, being printed makes the facts true | Only if the book is a comic or a joke book | Only if it is very new and very expensive
exercise: What are the four questions a good checker asks? | Who says it, how they know, when, and what others say | How long, how loud, how old, how pretty | Who likes it, who shares it, who saw it, and who paid for it | Where you are, what time, who's watching, why now
test: Finn says, "It's on a website so it's true." What's the best question to ask? | Who wrote it, and how do they know? | How many colours are on the website? | Is the website easy to read? | Has the website got lots of adverts?
test: "u said there's 8 planets but grandad's book says 9, ur wrong" What explains it? | The book is older than the 2006 decision about Pluto | Grandad's book is about a different solar system altogether | One of the planets was blown up by a comet quite recently | The printers made a mistake and added a planet by accident
test: An advert says a cream makes skin "ten years younger, clinically proven". What would a fair test need? | Many people, a comparison group, and honest measuring | Just one happy customer | A famous actor using it | A shiny bottle and a price
test: Yasmin is scared because her cat nibbled a poinsettia. What's true? | It may cause a mild upset; call the vet if worried, but it isn't deadly | The cat will die tonight | Poinsettias are completely harmless to every animal | Pets are never affected by any plants
test: Kai says "Wikipedia says it, so it's a fact." What's the clever reply? | Let's check the sources it links to at the bottom | Wikipedia is always lies | Wikipedia can't be edited by anyone, so it's always perfect | Only the first line of Wikipedia is true
test: "the newspaper says chocolate makes u clever!!!" The article says it was tested on 20 mice. What do you conclude? | The headline says much more than the study shows | Chocolate makes every person clever | Mice are clever only because they eat a lot of chocolate | Newspapers never make mistakes
test: Rhys says the zoo sign about white polar bear fur is fine. His mate says it's wrong because the hairs are colourless. Who is fairer? | Both: the fur looks white, though each hair is colourless | Only the mate: the sign is a lie | Only Rhys: the hairs are pure white, right down to the skin | Neither: polar bear fur is black
test: Beth says her brother's mate's dad at NASA says the Moon landing was faked. Which evidence matters more? | Mirrors left on the Moon that scientists still use today | What the dad told the brother's mate | How many people online believe it | That the photos look a bit strange
test: Two websites disagree about whether searing meat seals in juices. What's the best way to decide? | Look for one that tested it and explains how | Pick the one with the nicest and brightest pictures | Pick the one that your best friend likes the most | Pick the one that comes up first when you search
test: Jamie says printed books can't be wrong. Which example shows they can? | Old books listing nine planets | A book with a hard cover | A book with a picture of Pluto | A dictionary with many words
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the classification of Pluto since 2006. | It has been classified as a dwarf planet | It has been classified as the ninth planet | It has been classified as a moon of Neptune | It has been classified as a comet
exam: Identify the colour properly ascribed to the skin of the polar bear. | Black | White | Pink | Transparent
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the encyclopaedia Wikipedia as a source? | A useful starting point whose cited sources should be verified | An infallible authority incapable of error | A source invariably false in its entirety | A source that may on no account be edited by members of the public
exam: It is given that a newspaper headline asserts that chocolate renders persons more intelligent. Determine the most appropriate course of action. | Consult the article and ascertain the scale and subjects of the study | Accept the assertion upon the authority of the newspaper | Reject all newspapers as sources henceforth | Increase one's consumption of chocolate forthwith
